For a closed source client, yes, I agree. But that's not what I meant :) 
I mean a system that monitors the client in RAM, and ensures that it is 
what is expected. Commercial online games employ such systems. I don't 
know a lot about it, and it sounds hard. It also would be very platform 
dependant.

Actually, I'm not so sure it would be a big deal anyway. I *think* it 
would be only useful in avoiding rubber chicken related insta-YASDs or 
walking into known traps. These would occur for one of the following 
reasons:

a) Unspoiled newbie. Painful learning experience. Not fun. Noone here 
regards spoiling as cheating anyway.
b) Pressed wrong key. D'oh! The UI _should_ be helping to avoiding 
these.
c) Brain malfunction. Forgot not wearing gloves. A UI could easily 
highlight current wielded weapon and worn items. The existing Qt 
interface does.

In short, having the client prevent insta-YASDs is not such a big deal. 
For ease of ensuring a level playing field, insta-YASD prevention could 
even be implemented server side, although the message "Common sense 
prevents you from wielding the cockatrice corpse" is not very 
Nethackish. A (y/n) prompt would be more in keeping.


